### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to separate odd and even numbers using partitioningBy. This tests the distinction between partitioning and grouping, the guaranteed two-key result, and downstream collector nesting.

### How do you partition into odd and even?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class OddEvenPartition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> transactionIds = List.of(
            1001, 1002, 1003, 1004, 1005, 1006, 1007, 1008);

        Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> partition = transactionIds.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(n -> n % 2 == 0));

        System.out.println("Even: " + partition.get(true));
        System.out.println("Odd:  " + partition.get(false));
    }
}
```

```text
Even: [1002, 1004, 1006, 1008]
Odd:  [1001, 1003, 1005, 1007]
```

### Why partitioningBy instead of groupingBy on a boolean?

| Feature | partitioningBy | groupingBy(isEven) |
|---|---|---|
| Keys guaranteed | Both true and false always present | Missing key if no elements match |
| Null check needed | No | Yes, for missing key |
| Efficiency | Slightly optimized for binary split | General-purpose |

```java
// partitioningBy: both keys always exist
Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> partition = List.<Integer>of().stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(n -> n % 2 == 0));
System.out.println(partition.get(true));   // [] (empty list, not null)
System.out.println(partition.get(false));  // [] (empty list, not null)

// groupingBy on boolean: missing keys possible
Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> group = List.<Integer>of().stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(n -> n % 2 == 0));
System.out.println(group.get(true));   // null!
System.out.println(group.get(false));  // null!
```

### How do you nest a downstream collector?

```java
// Count per parity
Map<Boolean, Long> counts = transactionIds.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
        n -> n % 2 == 0,
        Collectors.counting()));

System.out.println("Even count: " + counts.get(true));
System.out.println("Odd count:  " + counts.get(false));
```

```text
Even count: 4
Odd count: 4
```

### How do you use partitioning for pass/fail classification?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ThresholdPartition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> balances = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("125000"),
            new BigDecimal("75000"),
            new BigDecimal("25000"));

        BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("50000");

        Map<Boolean, List<BigDecimal>> partition = balances.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                b -> b.compareTo(threshold) >= 0));

        System.out.println("Above threshold: " + partition.get(true));
        System.out.println("Below threshold: " + partition.get(false));
    }
}
```

```text
Above threshold: [50000, 125000, 75000]
Below threshold: [25000]
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using groupingBy when partitioningBy is more appropriate.

```java
// WRONG for binary split: groupingBy may have missing keys
Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> wrong = List.of(1, 3, 5).stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(n -> n % 2 == 0));
List<Integer> evens = wrong.get(true);  // null!

// CORRECT: partitioningBy guarantees both keys
Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> correct = List.of(1, 3, 5).stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(n -> n % 2 == 0));
List<Integer> safeEvens = correct.get(true);  // [] (empty, not null)
```

Mistake: confusing the parity test direction.

```java
// Check which key holds evens vs odds
// n % 2 == 0 → true = even, false = odd
// n % 2 != 0 → true = odd, false = even (reversed!)
```

### When should you use groupingBy instead?

Use groupingBy when there are more than two categories.

```java
// Three categories: negative, zero, positive
Map<String, List<Integer>> threeWay = List.of(-5, 0, 3, -2, 7).stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(n ->
        n < 0 ? "negative" : n == 0 ? "zero" : "positive"));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you partition employees by salary above/below a threshold?" Same pattern with a salary comparison predicate.
- "How do you get both the list and the count?" Use collectingAndThen or process the partition result.
- "Can you partition by more than one condition?" Not directly; use groupingBy with a multi-valued classifier.
